# Lifecycle-state JSONL logs are append-only by construction. The Tier-1
# `event-schema-valid` producer ensures every line is well-formed JSON, and
# both `lib/lifecycle-state.mjs::appendEvent` and
# `scripts/migrate-drift-fields.mjs` use `fs.appendFile` with `O_APPEND` so
# no in-place edits ever happen. Two concurrent feature branches that each
# append disjoint events to the same per-spec JSONL must produce a
# conflict-free three-way merge (this is the headline acceptance criterion
# of `jsonl-drift-events.spec.md` Behavior 8 — see also
# `tests/integration/spec-drift-no-merge-conflict.test.mjs`).
#
# `merge=union` tells git to keep both sides' added lines when the diff
# windows overlap, instead of marking the file as conflicted. Safe here
# because the per-spec JSONL has no "shared" lines either branch could
# meaningfully overwrite — every line is a discrete event.
.context-index/lifecycle-state/*.jsonl merge=union

# release-please bumps the "version" field of these three manifests
# independently on main and on release/next (see docs/releasing.md); the two
# lines are expected to diverge permanently. A plain merge conflicts on them
# any time main touches a nearby line (adjacent-hunk overlap). The
# `release-next-version` driver (scripts/git-merge-keep-version.sh)
# neutralizes only that known divergence before delegating to a normal
# 3-way merge, so a genuine conflict elsewhere in the file still surfaces.
# The driver is configured only in the propagate-to-next.yml workflow
# (`git config merge.release-next-version.driver ...`) — without that config
# these attributes are a no-op and git falls back to its default merge.
package.json merge=release-next-version
.claude-plugin/plugin.json merge=release-next-version
.cursor-plugin/plugin.json merge=release-next-version
